.TH TRK 1 "2026-10-15" "trk 0.4.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
trk \- adhd compliant task tracking
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trk
.RI [ global\-options ]
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ arguments ...]
.PP
With no command,
.B trk
prints the context view: the current task, its siblings, and its parents. With a
command it runs that command.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B trk
tracks what you are doing, and why, while your work branches into side quests.
It treats work as a tree of tasks under a goal, keeps a pointer to the one task
you are actually on, and makes it cheap to record, in the moment, how a new task
relates to the last one.
.PP
It is one binary and one plain text file. The file is human readable, hand
editable, safe to keep in git, and safe to back up with any tool. There is no
network access, no accounts, no background daemon, and no scheduling.
.SH CONCEPTS
.TP
.B Goal
A named "big thing" you are working toward, with its own tree of tasks and its
own cursor. Exactly one goal is active at a time.
.TP
.B Task
A single line of text with a state, a creation time, an ordered list of child
tasks, and a recorded duration. A child is "something I need in order to finish
the parent".
.TP
.B "Current task"
The task the active goal's cursor points at: what you are doing right now. It
receives no special treatment until you act on it with
.BR done ,
.BR time ,
.BR stop ,
or
.BR switch .
.TP
.B "Most recently added"
Every goal remembers the last task you added. The
.B then
and
.B and
commands anchor there, so a tree is built by chaining them.
.TP
.B State
One of open, done, dropped, stopped. Open and stopped tasks are unfinished; done
and dropped tasks are finished. Only
.B done
produces
.IR done ,
and
.B drop
removes a task outright.
.SH "THE MODEL"
Four commands add tasks, each relative to something that already exists.
.B add
adds a top-level task under the goal and moves the cursor onto it.
.B then
inserts a new task as the direct parent of the most recently added task, so the
old task (and everything under it) moves beneath the new one.
.B and
inserts a new task as a sibling immediately after the most recently added task,
and
.B before
inserts one immediately before it. That last pair is how you slot a step you
forgot between two tasks you already made.
.PP
.B then
and
.B and
leave the cursor where it is; only
.B add
moves it. That is what makes
.B "then \-n"
useful: it wraps the
.I current
task instead of the most recently added one.
.PP
.B add
,
.B then
,
.B and
, and
.B before
each accept an optional
.IR why :
.BR \-w / \-\-why
supplies it inline, otherwise
.B trk
asks
.BR "why? " \-\-
press Enter to record none, or pass
.B \-\-no\-why
to skip the prompt. The why is stored as a note and shown beneath the task by
.B list
and
.BR "progress goal" .
.PP
A typical forward plan starts with the first step and grows backward:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
trk add "Get staging creds"
trk then "Reproduce on staging"     # Reproduce is a parent of Get creds
trk then "Fix login bug"            # Fix login bug wraps Reproduce
trk and "Check auth logs"           # a sibling after Fix login bug
trk before "Ask for access"         # slots in before Check auth logs
.RE
.fi
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
.BR \-\-color " " \fIAUTO|ALWAYS|NEVER\fR
When to colorize output. Overrides the
.B color
config key. Also honoured:
.B NO_COLOR
(disable) and
.B TRK_ASCII=1
(ASCII symbols).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version.
.SH "COMMAND ALIASES"
.B d
for
.BR done ,
.B l
for
.BR list ,
.B u
for
.BR undo ,
and
.B i
for
.BR jot .
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "Adding tasks"
.TP
.BR "trk add " \fITEXT...\fR
Add a top-level task to the active goal. The new task becomes both the most
recently added and the current task.
.TP
.BR "trk then " [\-n|\-\-next] " " \fITEXT...\fR
Add a task as the direct parent of the most recently added task. With
.BR \-n / \-\-next ,
wrap the current task instead.
.TP
.BR "trk and " \fITEXT...\fR
Add a task as a sibling immediately after the most recently added task.
.TP
.BR "trk before " \fITEXT...\fR
Add a task as a sibling immediately before the most recently added task.
.PP
.B add
,
.BR then ,
.BR and ,
and
.B before
all accept
.BR \-w " " \fIWHY\fR
to record why the task exists, and
.B \-\-no\-why
to skip the prompt. Without
.BR \-w ,
.B trk
prompts
.B why?
when interactive; an empty answer records nothing.
.SS "Working a task"
.TP
.BR "trk list" " (alias " l )
Print the current task, its siblings, and up to three of its parents, with a
pointer at the current task. With no current task, the goal's top-level tasks
are shown.
.TP
.BR "trk switch"
Choose the current task with the picker, over the open tasks of the active goal.
.TP
.BR "trk done " [\-f|\-\-force]
Complete the current task and stop its timer. A task with open sub-tasks cannot
be completed until they are done; pass
.B \-\-force
to override, which exits 3 otherwise.
.TP
.BR "trk time"
Start a timer on the current task. If one is already running, the elapsed time
is reported instead.
.TP
.BR "trk stop"
Stop the timer on the current task and add the elapsed time to its total.
.B done
stops the timer too. With no timer running this exits 4.
.TP
.BR "trk drop " [\-y|\-\-yes]
Delete a task \-\- and its whole subtree \-\- using the picker. When the chosen
task has sub-tasks, a
.B y/n
confirmation is required; pass
.B \-\-yes
to skip it, which is required without a terminal. This is permanent; use
.B undo
to recover.
.TP
.BR "trk undo " [\fIN\fR]
Restore the store from the \fIN\fRth most recent snapshot (default 1) and print
the result.
.SS "Goals"
.TP
.BR "trk new goal " [\fITITLE...\fR]
Create a goal and make it active. The title is prompted for when omitted, which
requires a terminal.
.TP
.BR "trk list goal"
List every goal, numbered, with its open-task count and the active one marked.
.TP
.BR "trk switch goal"
Choose the active goal with the picker.
.TP
.BR "trk progress goal"
Print every task in the active goal as one tree. Completed tasks are struck
through, the current task is pointed at, and recorded durations are shown.
.TP
.BR "trk delete goal " [\-y|\-\-yes]
Delete a goal and all of its tasks, using the picker. A
.B y/n
confirmation is required unless
.B \-\-yes
is given.
.SS "Inbox"
.TP
.BR "trk jot " \fITEXT...\fR
Capture a task in the inbox without filing it under a goal.
.TP
.BR "trk inbox"
File inbox items with the picker. Choose an item, then a goal (TAB creates a new
goal), then optionally a task to nest under; the default files the item at the
top level of the goal.
.SS "Reporting"
.TP
.BR "trk log " [\fIWHEN\fR]
List what got finished, by day, newest first, with the time spent on each and a
daily total.
.I WHEN
is
.BR today " (the default), " yesterday ", a count of days back ("
.BR 7 ),
or a
.B YYYY\-MM\-DD
date.
.SS "Setup"
.TP
.BR "trk completions " [\fISHELL\fR]
Install shell completions for the detected shell (from
.BR $SHELL )
into the user's completion directory, creating it if needed and using
.B sudo
only when a system path is required. Pass a shell to override: one of
.BR sh ,
.BR bash ,
.BR zsh ,
.BR fish ,
.BR powershell ,
or
.BR elvish .
.SH "FIRST RUN"
The first time a command needs the store, and there is no config file,
.B trk
asks where to keep the task list and its backups, then writes the config. Set
.B TRK_STORE
to skip the prompts in a script.
.SH "A WORKED SESSION"
.nf
.RS 4
trk new goal "Ship login fix"
trk add  "Fix login bug"            # cursor moves here
trk then "Reproduce on staging"     # a parent of Fix login bug
trk add  "Get staging creds"        # a sibling line of work
trk list                            # where am I?
trk switch                          # pick a task to work on
trk time                            # start the clock
trk stop                            # pause, bank the time
trk done                            # finish, and stop the clock
trk progress goal                   # the whole tree
trk log 7                           # the last week
.RE
.fi
.SH "FILE FORMAT"
A store is UTF\-8 text. The first line is
.BR "trk 1" ,
the second is the id counters
.RB ( "next g=.. t=.. i=.." ),
and an optional third line names the active goal
.RB ( "active g1" ).
Blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored.
.PP
An optional
.B inbox
block lists captured items, each indented two spaces as
.BR "i<id> <timestamp> <text>" .
.PP
Each goal is a
.B goal
line:
.PP
.RS 4
.B goal g<id> <open|done> <timestamp> <title>
.RE
.PP
followed by indented lines: an optional
.B "current t<id>"
cursor and
.B "last t<id>"
pointer, and then the task tree. A task is:
.PP
.RS 4
.B "[ ] t<id> <timestamp> <text>"
.RE
.PP
where the state character is a space (open),
.B x
(done),
.B \-
(dropped), or
.B !
(stopped). Children are indented by two spaces. Attribute lines are indented two
spaces further and use
.B "@ key=value"
with keys
.BR done ,
.BR dropped ,
.BR at ,
.BR spent
(seconds), and
.BR running
(a start timestamp). Note lines begin with
.B "| "
and a stop reason with
.BR "! " .
.PP
Files written by older versions may still contain notes, stop reasons, and
schedules; they are preserved on read and write.
.PP
Writes are atomic: the new text is written to a temporary file, synced, and
renamed over the store, under an advisory lock.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The config file is TOML at
.BR $TRK_CONFIG ,
else
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/trk/config.toml ,
else
.BR $HOME/.config/trk/config.toml :
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
store         = "~/.local/share/trk/tasks.trk"
backups       = "~/.local/share/trk/backups"
color         = "auto"      # auto | always | never
wrap          = 80          # maximum output width
undo_depth    = 50          # undo snapshots kept
prompt_why    = true        # ask "why?" when adding a task
ascii         = "auto"      # auto | always | never
current_color = "green"     # color of the current task
.RE
.fi
.PP
Unknown keys are ignored with a warning.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TRK_CONFIG
Path to the config file.
.TP
.B TRK_STORE
Path to the store, overriding
.B store
in the config. Setting it with no config skips the first run.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable color.
.TP
.B TRK_ASCII
When set to
.BR 1 ,
use ASCII symbols instead of Unicode.
.SH "OUTPUT AND SYMBOLS"
All output wraps at the configured width (80 by default), never mid-character.
The current task is marked with
.B >
(ASCII) or a small triangle, the goal title is bold, the current task is bold in
.BR current_color ,
other tasks are dim green, and completed tasks are dim and struck through.
.PP
Without a terminal, a picker prints its numbered choices to standard error and
reads the number from standard input; an empty line accepts the highlighted
default. This lets
.BR switch ,
.BR drop ,
.BR delete goal ,
and
.B inbox
be driven from a script or pipeline.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
General error.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
Blocked (for example, \fBdone\fR on a task with open sub-tasks).
.TP
.B 4
Nothing to act on (no active goal, no current task, no timer, empty picker).
.TP
.B 5
Cancelled (a picker or confirmation was dismissed).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.config/trk/config.toml
Configuration.
.TP
.B ~/.local/share/trk/tasks.trk
Default store.
.TP
.B ~/.local/share/trk/backups
Default undo snapshots.
